Waymo Concierge
Narrative & PR-FAQ

Press Release
MOUNTAIN VIEW, CALIFORNIA — Waymo today announced the launch of Waymo Concierge, an in-app AI assistant that gives Waymo Pass subscribers instant, account-aware answers about their billing, mileage, and tier fit. Unlike generic chatbots that read from public help pages, Waymo Concierge is grounded in the subscriber's actual account — their current tier, miles used this cycle, recent charges, and the relevant Pass policy — and answers in under four seconds, with a confidence indicator and a one-tap path to a human rep when it is not sure.
When Waymo Pass launched, riders gained predictable monthly pricing in exchange for a fixed mileage cap. But that predictability came with new questions. Where am I in my mileage cap this month? Why was I charged an overage? Should I be on a different tier? Today, those questions go to email-based customer support and take a median of fourteen hours to resolve. For a subscriber paying eighty to two hundred dollars a month, that wait is a trust failure. Riders open the app, cannot get an answer, and lose confidence in the product they pay for.
Waymo Concierge closes that gap. Subscribers tap the Help icon in the Waymo app and ask in plain language: “How many miles do I have left this cycle?” “Why was I charged forty-two dollars last week?” “I have been driving more than I expected this month — what would the next tier up cost me?” Concierge looks up the subscriber's actual account, retrieves the relevant policy, and answers immediately, showing the data behind the answer.
Concierge is read-only by default. It can show subscribers everything about their account but cannot change anything without a separate, explicit confirmation. Tier changes, pauses, and refund requests use a preview-then-confirm pattern: Concierge shows exactly what will change, exactly when, and the subscriber taps a confirm button before anything happens. We never want a subscriber to feel an automated system did something to their account that they did not approve.
“I started using Waymo Pass when it launched, and the tier confusion was real — I had no idea if I should be paying for Flex or Daily, and I did not want to email support over and over to figure it out,” said Maya Chen, a Daily Commuter subscriber in San Francisco. “Concierge just shows me the math. Last month it told me I had used 240 miles on Daily, which is more than the Flex cap, so I would actually save money staying where I am. That is exactly what I wanted to know.”
Waymo Concierge launches today for all Waymo Pass subscribers in San Francisco, Phoenix, and Los Angeles, with rollout to additional Pass markets through Q3 2026.
External FAQ
Questions a subscriber, journalist, or partner might ask.
What can Waymo Concierge actually do?
It answers questions about your Waymo Pass account, including: how many miles you have used in your current cycle, why a specific charge appeared, what the difference between tiers means for someone with your usage pattern, what happens if you go over your cap, how to pause your subscription, and how to cancel. It can also walk you through what would change if you decided to switch tiers, before you make any change.
What will it not do?
It will not help with active rides — for those, the in-app safety button is always faster and goes straight to a human. It will not promise refunds or override Pass policy; refund requests are routed to a human rep with the full context of your conversation attached. It will not speculate about future pricing, and it will not make recommendations about whether Waymo is right for you compared to other rideshare options. If you ask something off-topic, it will point you to the right place — including a human rep when that is the right answer.
Will it tell me which tier I should be on?
It will show you the math, not make the call. Concierge surfaces your actual usage over the last 90 days and what each tier would have cost given that usage. You decide. We made this choice deliberately because a recommendation that fits your past behavior can age badly if your behavior changes the next month, and we do not want our agent making forward-looking claims about how you will use the service.
How accurate is it?
Every factual answer about your account is grounded in a tool call to your actual account data, not reasoned from training data. Every policy answer cites the specific policy section. We measure correctness against a curated test set of around 500 real subscriber questions and require correctness above 90% and hallucination below 1% before any change to the agent ships. If the agent is not confident in an answer, it tells you so and offers to route you to a human.
What if it gets something wrong?
Tell us through the “Was this helpful?” prompt at the end of every conversation. We review every flagged answer within 24 hours. If a wrong answer led to a charge or a tier change you did not authorize, we will fix it under our existing Waymo Pass support guarantee.
Is this just a re-skinned ChatGPT?
No. Concierge uses an LLM for reasoning and language, but every factual claim is grounded in your actual account and our actual Pass policy. It cannot invent a balance or hallucinate a refund policy because it does not have the latitude to. It is a narrow agent with read-only access to your account and a strict toolset, not an open-ended chatbot.
Can it actually make changes to my account?
Only with your explicit confirmation. If you ask to switch tiers, it shows you a preview — old tier, new tier, when the change takes effect, what happens to unused miles, what your next bill will look like — and the change happens only after you tap Confirm. The same pattern applies to pausing and canceling. The agent itself cannot move money or change your subscription state without that confirmation step.
Is my data private?
Yes. Concierge can read your account but does not share your data outside Waymo. Conversations are logged so we can improve the agent, but personally identifiable information is masked before any human at Waymo reviews a log. We do not use your conversation data to train models that serve other customers.
How do I get to it?
Tap the Help icon in the Waymo app. Available to all Waymo Pass subscribers in supported launch cities at no additional cost. The standard Contact a Rep option remains in the same place it has always been if you would rather skip the agent.
Internal FAQ
Questions a skeptical Waymo executive, finance partner, or legal counsel would ask.
What customer outcome are we actually moving?
Three numbers we already track: subscriber NPS, support tickets per active subscriber per month, and tier-fit churn (subscribers canceling within 90 days because they were on the wrong tier). Modeled outcomes assume 70% deflection of subscription-related tickets from human CS, $2.4M annual savings per 1M subscribers, and a 2-3 point NPS improvement driven by faster resolution. None of these is the brief's main metric (paid autonomous rides per week), but all of them feed it: subscribers who feel confident about their plan ride more, and subscribers who churn because they could not get a billing question answered ride zero.
Why now?
Two reasons. First, Waymo Pass is the strategic bet for converting frequent riders into habitual ones, and post-Pass support volume will grow faster than rides themselves. We cannot scale CS headcount linearly with subscribers and keep unit economics intact. Second, the LLM tooling has matured to the point where the failure modes are now characterizable and testable. We have a defensible eval framework — see the accompanying Experimentation Exercise — that catches the categories of error we care about (hallucinated balances, unauthorized state changes, PII leaks) before a user sees them.
Why are we building this versus buying it?
Off-the-shelf agent platforms are open-domain by design and assume the operator wants a flexible “ask anything” experience. We want the opposite: a tightly scoped agent with a known tool surface, explicit refusal patterns, and a hallucination-rate bar we set ourselves. The build cost (one agent engineer plus one PM for roughly eight weeks) is materially below the cost of integrating, customizing, and trusting a third-party product with subscriber billing data.
What is the brand risk if we ship this and get it wrong?
A single high-profile billing hallucination is a Twitter-grade event for a brand whose entire positioning is trust. We mitigate four ways. (1) Every account claim is grounded in a tool call, with the answer flagging which fields it pulled. (2) Every state-changing action requires a confirmation step enforced at the tool layer, not by the LLM's promise. (3) The agent has explicit confidence indicators and a “verify with a human” path below threshold. (4) We ship by city, mirroring Waymo Pass rollout discipline, so a regression hits a contained population first. The kill criteria below give us a reversal path.
Why include tier recommendations? Is it not the riskiest feature?
Yes, and it is the one we revised most heavily. The original spec had the agent recommending a tier directly (“you should switch to Flex”). We rejected that. The final spec has the agent surface usage data and what each tier would have cost given that usage, and asks the subscriber what they want to do. This addresses the failure mode where a recommendation ages badly because the user's behavior changed. We do not want the agent making any forward-looking claim about a customer's future usage. The agent shows the math; the customer makes the call.
Why is the agent not allowed to approve refunds?
Because refunds touch retention strategy, fraud signals, and policy edge cases that we do not want an agent making judgment calls on. Concierge can file a refund request — collect the customer's reasoning, attach the relevant transactions and account context, and route the package to a human queue with a recommended disposition — but a human approves. This is a deliberate scope cut. The agent never tells a customer their refund is approved.
Will this just push the work to humans when it fails?
That is partly the point. The goal is for easy, high-volume questions to deflect cleanly and for hard or ambiguous ones to escalate with full context. Today, every question is a human ticket, including “how many miles do I have left.” We expect Concierge to take roughly 70% of question volume off the human queue and to hand off the remaining 30% better than a cold ticket would arrive — with the question summarized, the relevant account context attached, and the agent's reasoning visible to the rep.
What is the cost?
Inference target: $0.04 per resolved conversation, versus roughly $3 for a human-handled ticket. Engineering: one agent engineer and one PM for eight weeks to first launch, plus ongoing eval-set maintenance at roughly 0.25 FTE indefinitely. Infra costs are shared with the existing Waymo app stack. At our 1M-subscriber projection in launch cities, total run-rate cost is well under the deflection savings.
What is the hallucination tolerance and how is it measured?
Below 1% on the Eval Set is the bar before any change ships. Hallucination is defined as a factual claim about the user's account or about Pass policy that is not supported by tool output or retrieved policy text. We use a grounded-fact checker that verifies every factual span in the response against tool results and retrieved chunks, plus a 10% human spot-check on production traffic. If the rate spikes above 1% on a model update, the agent rolls back to the prior version automatically.
Why not voice? Why not multi-language at launch?
Both are roadmapped, neither is launch. Voice opens up safety-while-driving questions we want to handle separately and would expand the eval surface area we cannot validate before our city rollout window. Multi-language adds the same kind of eval surface we cannot validate in time. English-only at launch in San Francisco, Phoenix, and Los Angeles. Spanish in Q3.
What is the kill criteria?
We pause Concierge in a city if any of the following holds: (a) hallucination rate above 1% on the Eval Set after a model update, (b) any unconfirmed state-changing tool call detected in production logs, (c) post-Concierge subscriber NPS in a launch city drops by more than 2 points relative to the same month last year, or (d) the refund-dispute rate from Concierge-handled conversations exceeds the rate from human-handled tickets by more than 25%.
What is the legal and regulatory exposure?
Reviewed with Legal in advance. Concierge does not provide financial or legal advice, does not make eligibility determinations, does not handle disputes that would otherwise go to formal CFPB or state-level processes (those route to humans), and does not store or transmit payment card data. The click-to-cancel path is one tap inside the agent and meets the FTC requirement for a one-step cancel option. PII handling is covered by the existing Waymo Pass DPA.
How does this connect to the post-ride upsell experiment?
The post-ride contextual upsell brings new subscribers in with a personalized savings claim, anchored to actual usage. Concierge owns the post-conversion experience — making sure the new subscriber understands their plan, can verify the savings claim was accurate, and can change tiers cleanly if their behavior shifts. The upsell brings them in. Concierge is part of why they stay.
Appendix: “So What?” Test
Per our PM Operating Manual, every paragraph in this document is gut-checked against “what is the concrete customer benefit?” Summary:
Press release lead: Subscriber gets account-grounded answers in seconds instead of waiting 14 hours for an email reply.
Read-only by default: Subscriber never has an automated system change their account without a deliberate confirmation step.
Tier math, not tier rec: Subscriber gets the data they need to decide and is not blamed for following an agent's stale recommendation.
Confidence indicator: Subscriber knows when to trust the answer versus when to escalate, instead of having to guess.
Refund request, not approval: Subscriber gets a fast capture of their issue with full context routed to a human, instead of a synthetic answer the human will then have to undo.
Kill criteria + per-city rollout: If something breaks, it breaks in one city for one cohort, not across the entire subscriber base.
